WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.152
Why chunk at all.

2
00:00:01.432 --> 00:00:03.971
After ingestion you have clean documents.

3
00:00:04.251 --> 00:00:13.680
You could embed each whole document as one vector, but three things push you to cut them into chunks, pieces of a few hundred to a couple of thousand tokens each.

4
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.480
First, a vector summarizes everything it was computed from.

5
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:29.643
One vector for a 30-page handbook averages vacation rules, safety rules, and expense rules into a single point, and a question about sick days is only weakly similar to that average.

6
00:00:29.923 --> 00:00:32.931
A vector for the sick-days paragraph is strongly similar.

7
00:00:33.211 --> 00:00:35.280
Smaller chunks give sharper matches.

8
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:41.576
Second, embedding models have input limits; anything past the limit is truncated or rejected.

9
00:00:41.856 --> 00:00:45.803
Third, what you retrieve goes into a prompt, and the prompt has a budget.

10
00:00:46.083 --> 00:01:04.152
Retrieving five 300-token chunks costs 1,500 tokens; retrieving five whole documents might cost 100,000, and long, padded contexts make it harder for a model to use what matters (recall the “Lost in the Middle” findings from Studio 1).

11
00:01:04.432 --> 00:01:06.331
The opposing force is context.

12
00:01:06.611 --> 00:01:17.043
Cut too small and a chunk loses the information that made it answerable: “It must be approved by a director” is useless if “it” was defined two paragraphs earlier.

13
00:01:17.323 --> 00:01:27.136
Every strategy in this lesson is a different answer to the same question: how do you make pieces small enough to match precisely and large enough to mean something on their own?

14
00:01:27.416 --> 00:01:33.944
A practical unit note: chunk sizes are quoted in tokens, characters, or words, depending on the tool.

15
00:01:34.224 --> 00:01:38.491
LangChain’s recursive splitter measures characters by default, for example.

16
00:01:38.771 --> 00:01:47.496
Roughly four characters per English token is a fine conversion for planning, but measure with your embedding model’s tokenizer when the limit matters.
